  "title": "Searching text",
  "summary": "Ask if text contains something, where it is, how many times it appears, and what kind of characters it has.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 25,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "expr",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "Python has ready-made tools for searching inside a string:\n\n- `part in text` is **True** or **False**. `not in` is the opposite.\n- `text.find(part)` gives the **position** where `part` first starts, or **-1** when it is not there.\n- `text.count(part)` says **how many times** `part` appears.\n- `text.startswith(part)` and `text.endswith(part)` check the two ends.\n- `text.isdigit()` is True when every character is a digit, so `int(text)` will work. `text.isalpha()` is True when every character is a letter.\n\n```\nnote = \"meet at noon\"\nprint(\"noon\" in note)\nprint(note.find(\"at\"))\n```\n\nprints True and then 5.\n\nAll of these are **case sensitive**: \"Noon\" is not \"noon\". Use `lower()` on the text first when case should not matter. And watch out for -1 from find: it is a normal number, not False.",
    "analogy": "The search box in a document: is the word there at all, where is the first hit, and how many hits are there?"
  },
